{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>וְלֹא אִתְּכֶם לְבַדְּכֶם וְגוֹ׳ כִּי אֶת אֲשֶׁר וְגוֹ׳.</b> It is difficult: why was it necessary to say ״וְלֹא אִתְּכֶם״ etc., and it was not sufficient for Him to say ״אֶת אֲשֶׁר יֶשְׁנוֹ״ etc. — for they are those whom He was speaking with — ״וְאֵת אֲשֶׁר״ etc., and it is self-evident that He is making the covenant not with them alone etc. However, the intent of the verse is that He comes to obligate them to accept upon themselves the covenant and the imprecation, and upon their descendants who come after them forever; and that is His saying <b>וְלֹא אִתְּכֶם לְבַדְּכֶם</b> — meaning, to obligate you alone I am not making [the covenant], but rather both those who are with us and those etc. And consequently, had He said only ״אֶת אֲשֶׁר וְגוֹ׳ וְאֶת אֲשֶׁר וְגוֹ׳ אָנֹכִי כּוֹרֵת״, it would have conveyed only that HaShem obligates also those who come after them, but He does not obligate those who are standing there, on behalf of their descendants after them; and by His saying ״לֹא אִתְּכֶם לְבַדְּכֶם״ He revealed His intent that in the imprecation itself which He makes with those standing there, He adds to obligate them for the generations. And the parsing of the verse is: <b>לֹא אִתְּכֶם לְבַדְּכֶם</b> do I obligate regarding what is stated, but also regarding the generations that come after you — and this would not have been conveyed had He said only ״אֶת אֲשֶׁר וְגוֹ׳ וְאֵת אֲשֶׁר וְגוֹ׳ אָנֹכִי כּוֹרֵת בְּרִית,״ for I would have said that these are words of HaShem regarding those who come after them, not that those standing there would be obligated to accept upon themselves on behalf of the children."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:20:03.749540835Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}